## Why This Question Is Asked

This problem tests your ability to combine multiple data structures to solve a real-world analytics challenge: maintaining sliding window aggregations with fast top-K retrieval under tight latency constraints.

## Key Concepts

1. **Circular bucket array** enables O(1) bucketing and automatic expiration of old events
2. **Hashmap aggregation** provides O(1) lookup and update for per-user totals across categories
3. **Max-heap with lazy deletion** avoids the overhead of rebuilding the heap on every expiration; stale entries are filtered on-demand during `topK`
4. **Lazy expiration** ensures we never scan individual events during query time—only expired buckets trigger cleanup

## Code Example

```javascript
class WindowedTopK {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.buckets = new Array(W).fill(null).map(() => ({ head: null, tail: null, ts: null }));
    this.totals = new Map();
    this.heap = [];
    this.head = 0;
  }

  insertEvent(ts, userId, category, value) {
    const idx = ts % this.W;
    const bucket = this.buckets[idx];
    const event = { userId, category, value, ts, next: null };
    if (!bucket.head) bucket.head = bucket.tail = event;
    else bucket.tail = bucket.tail.next = event;
    bucket.ts = ts;

    this.totals.set(userId, (this.totals.get(userId) || 0) + value);
    this.heap.push({ total: this.totals.get(userId), userId, ts });
  }

  advanceTime(now) {
    const cutoff = now - this.W;
    while (this.buckets[this.head].ts !== null && this.buckets[this.head].ts < cutoff) {
      let event = this.buckets[this.head].head;
      while (event) {
        this.totals.set(event.userId, (this.totals.get(event.userId) || 0) - event.value);
        event = event.next;
      }
      this.buckets[this.head] = { head: null, tail: null, ts: null };
      this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.W;
    }
  }

  topK(now, K) {
    this.advanceTime(now);
    const result = [];
    let i = 0;
    while (result.length < K && i < this.heap.length) {
      const entry = this.heap[i++];
      if (this.totals.get(entry.userId) === entry.total) {
        result.push(entry.userId);
      }
    }
    return result;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- **Per-category top-K**: Maintain separate totals per `(userId, category)` and separate heaps for each category
- **Bounded memory**: Add a periodic rebuild phase or use LRU eviction for rarely-active user_ids
- **Distributed extension**: Partition by user_id hash across nodes, aggregate partial results

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to model DP with obstacles and variable step sizes, requiring careful base cases and transition logic. It also assesses both memoization and tabulation familiarity.

## Key Concepts

- DP with multiple transitions (1..k)
- Handling blocked steps
- Memoization vs tabulation
- Time complexity: O(n*maxJump); Space: O(n) (or O(maxJump) with optimization)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Bottom-up tabulation
function countWays(n, maxJump, blocked) {
  const dp = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    if (blocked[i]) { dp[i] = 0; continue; }
    for (let j = 1; j <= maxJump; j++) {
      if (i - j >= 0) dp[i] += dp[i - j];
    }
  }
  return dp[n];
}
```

```javascript
// Memoized recursive version
function countWaysRec(n, maxJump, blocked, i = n, memo = {}) {
  if (i === 0) return 1;
  if (i < 0 || blocked[i]) return 0;
  if (memo[i] !== undefined) return memo[i];
  let ways = 0;
  for (let t = 1; t <= maxJump; t++) ways += countWaysRec(n, maxJump, blocked, i - t, memo);
  memo[i] = ways;
  return ways;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize to O(maxJump) space with a sliding window?
- How would you adjust for modulo arithmetic to prevent overflow for large n?

## Why This Is Asked

Understanding three-way partitioning shows depth beyond basic quicksort, especially for duplicates. It also tests in-place mutation and careful loop invariants.

## Key Concepts

- Three-way partitioning around a pivot
- In-place sorting with O(1) extra space
- Average vs worst-case time, handling duplicates

## Code Example

```javascript
function threeWayQuicksort(arr, left=0, right=arr.length-1) {
  if (left >= right) return;
  const pivot = arr[Math.floor((left + right) / 2)];
  let lt = left, i = left, gt = right;
  while (i <= gt) {
    if (arr[i] < pivot) { [arr[lt], arr[i]] = [arr[i], arr[lt]]; lt++; i++; }
    else if (arr[i] > pivot) { [arr[i], arr[gt]] = [arr[gt], arr[i]]; gt--; }
    else { i++; }
  }
  threeWayQuicksort(arr, left, lt - 1);
  threeWayQuicksort(arr, gt + 1, right);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you mitigate worst-case performance in practice?
- How would you adapt the algorithm to a custom comparator or stable variants?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests per-key streaming stats with independent sliding windows, combining deques, hashtables, and a heap with lazy expirations. It mirrors real services that analyze per-entity signals under time constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Per-key deques for time-bounded data
- Hash map for key -> stats
- Max-heap with lazy deletion to keep top averages up-to-date
- Expiration handling driven by currentTime

## Code Example
```javascript
class PerKeyRollingStats {
  constructor(W) { this.W = W; this.map = new Map(); this.heap = new MaxHeap(); }
  add(key, value, ts) {
    let rec = this.map.get(key) || { dq: [], sum: 0 };
    rec.dq.push([ts, value]);
    rec.sum += value;
    while (rec.dq.length && rec.dq[0][0] < ts - this.W) {
      const [t, v] = rec.dq.shift();
      rec.sum -= v;
    }
    this.map.set(key, rec);
    const avg = rec.sum / rec.dq.length;
    this.heap.push({ key, avg, validAt: ts, len: rec.dq.length });
  }
  topK(currentTime, K) {
    const res = [];
    while (res.length < K) {
      const top = this.heap.pop();
      if (!top) break;
      const rec = this.map.get(top.key);
      const avg = rec && rec.dq.length ? rec.sum / rec.dq.length : -Infinity;
      if (top.avg !== avg) continue; // stale entry
      res.push(top.key);
    }
    // optional: reinsert from a temp to preserve heap state
    return res;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle a very large number of keys with memory constraints?
- How would you adapt to variable window sizes per key or out-of-order events?

## Why This Question Is Asked
This problem evaluates the ability to extend classic DP patterns (like the staircase problem) with an additional resource dimension, while also testing space optimization skills and recursive thinking via memoization. It distinguishes candidates who understand both bottom-up and top-down approaches, and who reason through edge cases systematically.

## Key Concepts
- DP with an extra budget constraint (two-dimensional state: position + accumulated cost)
- Space optimization using rolling arrays to reduce O(n·B) space to O(B)
- Memoization on composite state (i, b) for the recursive variant
- Correct base case handling when starting from a virtual position before index 0

## Code Example
```javascript
const MOD = 1_000_000_007n;

function countWays(cost, B) {
  const n = cost.length;
  if (n === 0) return 0;

  // bottom-up with rolling arrays
  let prev2 = new Array(B + 1).fill(0);
  let prev1 = new Array(B + 1).fill(0);

  // base: step 0
  if (cost[0] <= B) prev2[cost[0]] = 1;

  // base: step 1
  if (n > 1 && cost[1] <= B) prev1[cost[1]] = 1;

  // step 2 onwards
  for (let i = 2; i < n; i++) {
    const cur = new Array(B + 1).fill(0);
    for (let b = cost[i]; b <= B; b++) {
      cur[b] = (prev1[b - cost[i]] + prev2[b - cost[i]]) % MOD;
    }
    prev2 = prev1;
    prev1 = cur;
  }

  return n === 1 ? prev2[B] : prev1[B];
}

// memoized DFS variant
function countWaysDFS(cost, B) {
  const n = cost.length;
  const memo = new Map();

  function dfs(i, b) {
    if (b < 0) return 0;
    if (i < 0) return b === 0 ? 1 : 0;
    const key = `${i},${b}`;
    if (memo.has(key)) return memo.get(key);
    const result = (dfs(i - 1, b - cost[i]) + dfs(i - 2, b - cost[i])) % MOD;
    memo.set(key, result);
    return result;
  }

  return dfs(n - 1, B);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Quicksort with many duplicates can degrade; 3-way partitioning reduces swaps and comparisons.

## Key Concepts

- 3-way partitioning (Dutch National Flag)
- In-place iterative sorting (no recursion)
- Median-of-three pivot to avoid degeneracy
- Space: O(log n) for manual stack

## Code Example

```javascript
function quicksort3WayIterative(arr) {
  const stack = [];
  stack.push([0, arr.length - 1]);
  while (stack.length) {
    const [l, r] = stack.pop();
    if (l >= r) continue;
    const m = l + Math.floor((r - l) / 2);
    // median-of-three to pick pivot
    if (arr[l] > arr[m]) [arr[l], arr[m]] = [arr[m], arr[l]];
    if (arr[l] > arr[r]) [arr[l], arr[r]] = [arr[r], arr[l]];
    if (arr[m] > arr[r]) [arr[m], arr[r]] = [arr[r], arr[m]];
    const pivot = arr[m];
    // Dutch National Flag partition
    let i = l, lt = l, gt = r;
    while (i <= gt) {
      if (arr[i] < pivot) { [arr[lt], arr[i]] = [arr[i], arr[lt]]; lt++; i++; }
      else if (arr[i] > pivot) { [arr[i], arr[gt]] = [arr[gt], arr[i]]; gt--; }
      else { i++; }
    }
    stack.push([l, lt - 1]);
    stack.push([gt + 1, r]);
  }
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you verify stability with duplicates in this approach?
- How would you adapt to 64-bit integers and different memory constraints?

## Why This Is Asked
In practice, data often contains many duplicates. A 3-way partitioned quicksort minimizes comparisons on duplicates and scales with the number of distinct keys.

## Key Concepts
- Three-way partitioning (Dutch National Flag)
- Randomized pivot to avoid worst-case
- Complexity: O(N log D) expected when D distinct keys
- In-place sort with minimal extra space

## Code Example
```javascript
function sort3(a, l=0, r=a.length-1){
  if (l >= r) return;
  const pivot = a[Math.floor(Math.random()*(r-l+1))+l];
  let i=l, lt=l, gt=r;
  while (i <= gt){
    if (a[i] < pivot){ [a[lt], a[i]] = [a[i], a[lt]]; lt++; i++; }
    else if (a[i] > pivot){ [a[i], a[gt]] = [a[gt], a[i]]; gt--; }
    else { i++; }
  }
  sort3(a, l, lt-1);
  sort3(a, gt+1, r);
}
```

