- What are the trade-offs vs a counting/radix approach on integers?

## Why This Is Asked

This question combines multiple data structure concepts: per-user sliding windows within a global top-K context. It integrates arrays (per-user queues), hash tables for aggregates, and heaps for top-K retrieval with lazy invalidation. The problem tests a candidate's ability to:
- Design composite data structures for streaming workloads
- Handle time-based expirations efficiently
- Reason about algorithmic complexity under constraints
- Manage edge cases in production systems

## Key Concepts

1. **Sliding window per user** — Each user maintains their own event history, allowing O(1) expiration of old events via queue front-draining.
2. **Lazy invalidation** — The heap never explicitly deletes stale entries; instead, we check and skip them during retrieval. This keeps `addEvent` at O(log n) without expensive heap repairs.
3. **Aggregate maps** — Fast O(1) lookup and update of per-user totals, avoiding full recalculation.
4. **Tie-breaking** — Using `(delta, user_id)` as a composite key ensures stable, deterministic ordering.

## Code Example

```javascript
class RollingActiveUsers {
  constructor(windowSeconds) {
    this.W = windowSeconds;
    this.userWindows = new Map();
    this.userTotals = new Map();
    this.maxHeap = new MaxHeap();
  }

  addEvent(ts, userId, delta) {
    if (!this.userWindows.has(userId)) {
      this.userWindows.set(userId, []);
      this.userTotals.set(userId, 0);
    }
    this.userWindows.get(userId).push({ ts, delta });
    const newTotal = this.userTotals.get(userId) + delta;
    this.userTotals.set(userId, newTotal);
    this.maxHeap.push({ delta: newTotal, userId });
    this.expireIfNeeded(userId, ts);
  }

  expireIfNeeded(userId, now) {
    const cutoff = now - this.W;
    const events = this.userWindows.get(userId);
    while (events.length && events[0].ts < cutoff) {
      const expired = events.shift();
      this.userTotals.set(userId, this.userTotals.get(userId) - expired.delta);
    }
  }

  topKUsers(k, now) {
    const results = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < k; i++) {
      const top = this.maxHeap.pop();
      if (!top || !this.isValid(top, now)) break;
      results.push(top.userId);
      this.maxHeap.push(top);
    }
    return results;
  }

  isValid(entry, now) {
    const currentTotal = this.userTotals.get(entry.userId);
    return currentTotal !== undefined && Math.abs(currentTotal - entry.delta) < 0.0001;
  }
}
```

## Complexity Analysis

| Operation | Time | Space |
|-----------|------|-------|
| `addEvent` | O(log n) | O(n) |
| `topKUsers` | O(K log n) | O(K) |
| Expiration | O(m) amortized per event, where m = events expired | — |

## Common Pitfalls

- **Forgetting to re-push extracted heap entries** — corrupts the heap state
- **Not handling negative totals** — users with negative deltas are valid
- **Skipping expiration check in `topKUsers`** — stale heap entries will produce wrong results
- **Memory leaks** — empty deques should be removed from the map

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP state design, memoization vs tabulation, and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions; state i is start index; transitions extend end while sum <= limit.
- Prefix sums for O(1) range sums; reconstruction via choice pointers.
- Edge cases: impossible when an element exceeds limit; handling ties.

## Code Example
```javascript
function minPartitionCost(nums, limit){
  const n = nums.length, pref=[0];
  for(const x of nums) pref.push(pref[pref.length-1]+x);
  const memo = new Array(n+1).fill(null);
  function dfs(i){
    if(i===n) return [0,[]];
    if(memo[i]) return memo[i];
    let best=[Infinity, null];
    for(let j=i;j<n;j++){ const sum=pref[j+1]-pref[i]; if(sum>limit) break; const sub=dfs(j+1); const cost=(j-i+1)**2+sub[0]; if(cost<best[0]) best=[cost, [i,j].concat(sub[1]||[])]; }
    memo[i]=best; return best;
  }
  return dfs(0);
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you modify to also return the actual blocks boundaries when multiple optimal partitions exist?
- How does the solution change if limit is dynamic per block?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of two classic sorts, practical optimization, and memory trade-offs. A hybrid approach reveals ability to tune algorithms for real data patterns.

## Key Concepts
- Hybrid sorting threshold and when it helps
- Stability via merge vs insertion behavior
- Space usage with a single auxiliary buffer

## Code Example
```javascript
function sortBatch(arr) {
  const n = arr.length;
  const buf = new Array(n);
  function insertionSort(a, left, right) {
    for (let i = left + 1; i <= right; i++) {
      const key = a[i];
      let j = i - 1;
      while (j >= left && a[j] > key) {
        a[j + 1] = a[j];
        j--;
      }
      a[j + 1] = key;
    }
  }
  function merge(left, mid, right) {
    let i = left, j = mid + 1, k = left;
    while (i <= mid && j <= right) {
      if (arr[i] <= arr[j]) buf[k++] = arr[i++];
      else buf[k++] = arr[j++];
    }
    while (i <= mid) buf[k++] = arr[i++];
    while (j <= right) buf[k++] = arr[j++];
    for (let t = left; t <= right; t++) arr[t] = buf[t];
  }
  function sort(l, r) {
    if (l >= r) return;
    if (r - l + 1 <= 16) {
      insertionSort(arr, l, r);
    } else {
      const m = Math.floor((l + r) / 2);
      sort(l, m);
      sort(m + 1, r);
      merge(l, m, r);
    }
  }
  sort(0, n - 1);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of Trie data structures, deduplication strategies, and efficient prefix queries—essential skills for building autocomplete systems.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with per-node subtree counts for O(1) prefix counting
- Deduplication via global set to maintain word uniqueness
- Prefix-based retrieval with controlled result limits
- Lexicographic ordering through systematic child traversal

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.isWord = false;
    this.passCount = 0;
  }
}

class Trie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
    this.wordSet = new Set();
  }

  insert(word) {
    if (this.wordSet.has(word)) return;
    this.wordSet.add(word);
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      if (!node.next.has(char)) {
        node.next.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.next.get(char);
      node.passCount++;
    }
    node.isWord = true;
  }

  countPrefix(prefix) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      if (!node.next.has(char)) return 0;
      node = node.next.get(char);
    }
    return node.passCount;
  }

  listWithPrefix(prefix, limit) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      if (!node.next.has(char)) return [];
      node = node.next.get(char);
    }
    const result = [];
    this.dfs(node, prefix, result, limit);
    return result;
  }

  dfs(node, current, result, limit) {
    if (result.length >= limit) return;
    if (node.isWord) {
      result.push(current);
    }
    const sortedKeys = Array.from(node.next.keys()).sort();
    for (const char of sortedKeys) {
      this.dfs(node.next.get(char), current + char, result, limit);
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to combine hash maps, deques, and heaps to implement a sliding-window top-K feature in real time.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window with per-player aggregates
- Lazy deletions in a heap to avoid frequent rebalancing
- Expiration management via per-player deques
- Trade-offs between memory usage and update/query latency

## Code Example
```javascript
class Leaderboard {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.map = new Map();
    this.heap = [];
  }

  addEvent(pid, delta, ts) {
    const record = this.map.get(pid) || { sum: 0, deque: [] };
    record.deque.push({ ts, delta });
    record.sum += delta;

    while (record.deque.length && record.deque[0].ts < ts - this.W) {
      record.sum -= record.deque.shift().delta;
    }

    this.map.set(pid, record);
    this.heap.push({ sum: record.sum, pid });
  }

  topK(now, K) {
    const results = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < K; i++) {
      while (this.heap.length) {
        const top = this.heap[0];
        const record = this.map.get(top.pid);
        if (!record || record.sum !== top.sum) {
          heapPop(this.heap);
        } else {
          results.push(this.heapPop(this.heap).pid);
          break;
        }
      }
    }
    return results;
  }
}
```

## Data Structure Overview
| Component | Purpose | Complexity |
|-----------|---------|-------------|
| Hash Map | O(1) lookup per player | O(n) memory |
| Per-player Deque | Track event history, expire old entries | O(1) amortized push/shift |
| Max-Heap | Maintain top players efficiently | O(log n) push, O(1) peek |

## Time & Space Complexity
- **addEvent**: O(log n) amortized—each event enters and leaves the deque at most once
- **topK**: O(K log n)—each invalid entry is popped at most once across all queries
- **Memory**: O(n) for the hash map and deques; O(n) worst-case for the heap

## Follow-up Questions
- How to tighten the lazy deletion cost when the heap grows large?
- Can a segment tree or Fenwick tree provide stricter time guarantees?
- How would you handle concurrent updates from multiple streams?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design both memoized and tabulated DP for a non-trivial partition constraint, reinforcing DP patterns and edge-case handling.

## Key Concepts
- Top-down memoization with arrays
- Bottom-up tabulation
- Prefix sums for O(1) range sums
- Power-of-two check with bit trick

## Code Example
```javascript
function countPartitions(nums){
  const n = nums.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) pref[i+1]=pref[i]+nums[i];
  const isPow2 = (x)=> x>0 && (x&(x-1))===0;
  // Memoized top-down
  const memo = new Array(n+1).fill(-1);
  const top = (i)=>{
    if(i===n) return 1;
    if(memo[i]!==-1) return memo[i];
    let res = 0;
    for(let j=i+1;j<=n;j++){
      const s = pref[j]-pref[i];
      if(isPow2(s)) res += top(j);
    }
    memo[i]=res; return res;
  };
  // Bottom-up
  const dp = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  dp[n]=1;
  for(let i=n-1;i>=0;i--){
    let sumWays=0;
    for(let j=i+1;j<=n;j++){
      const s = pref[j]-pref[i];
      if(isPow2(s)) sumWays += dp[j];
    }
    dp[i]=sumWays;
  }
  return {topWays: top(0), bottomWays: dp[0]};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize for large n or multiple queries on the same array?
- How would you handle large counts and modulo arithmetic to prevent overflow?

## Why This Is Asked
Autocompletion is common in editors and apps; this tests a lightweight data structure for fast prefix queries and shows practical space/time trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Trie structure with per-node prefix counts
- O(len(prefix)) lookup
- Space proportional to total characters in dictionary
- Edge cases: empty prefix, missing path

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.count = 0;
  }
}
class Trie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
  }
  insert(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) node.children.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      node = node.children.get(ch);
      node.count++;
    }
  }
  countWithPrefix(prefix) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of prefix) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) return 0;
      node = node.children.get(ch);
    }
    return node.count;
  }
}
```

